The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\mathbf{c}=\overrightarrow{OC}$.

![Vector diagram](images/40/40fa086b65b7205090c58675ce7a8387ee75591011c6fa6ebf2db04867d43f8e.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
![Completed vector route](images/4b/4b0dd2524c38644e9bfdeb3262642832cad9d80f36b240c5543bf64772dc8758.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $2$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares down. Thus $OX=1|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $6$ squares right and $6$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares right and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=3|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=3\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=\mathbf{b}+3\mathbf{a}\\&=3\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=3$ and $\mu=1$.

